  "query_preview": "..The ridge aloft that's been driving our hot \ntemperatures continues shifting to the east with the ridge axis now \nalong the AZ/NM border. This position is allowing southerly flow \naloft between the ridge and a trough off central Baja. This flow is \nimporting a bit of increased mid level moisture into southeast \nArizona with PWAT's currently around 0.6 inches and expected to \nnudge higher today. While the main story of the day will continue to \nbe the hot temperatures with highs in the triple digits from Tucson \nwestward and in the mid to upper 90s south and east of Tucson, we'll \nsee some cloud build-ups today from Tucson eastward. Latest \nHREF/HRRR/CAMs show areas of virga with a slight chance of a dry \nlightning strike or two. The main concern will be some gusty outflow \nwinds, and the HREF does indicate localized areas of neighborhood \nprobabilities of 30 to 50 percent of wind gusts in excess of 30 kts \neast of Tucson. Any cloud build-ups/virga is expected to diminish \nthis evening after sunset. \n\nFor Wednesday, the aforementioned trough off central Baja will shift \ntowards the NNE with southwesterly flow aloft resuming across our \nforecast area. This will effectively start shunting the higher mid \nlevel moisture towards the east with similar virga/dry thunderstorm \nconcerns to today more limited to Graham/Greenlee/Cochise Counties \ncloser to the NM border. Additionally, southwesterly winds will \nincrease to around 15 to 20 mph with gusts to around 25 to 30 mph. \nTemperatures will drop a couple of degrees compared to today but \nstill well above normal. \n\nFor Thursday through Saturday, dry conditions prevail with west to \nsouthwest flow with the main storm track well to our north. Height \nand thickness values lower slightly during this period allowing for \na few degrees of cooling but temperatures will still be around 3 to \n6 degrees above normal. Winds remain breezy on Thursday south and \neast of Tucson but otherwise just some typical afternoon \nbreezes/gustiness Friday and Saturday.\n\nBeyond Saturday there is a bit more uncertainty on the southern \nextent of another potential trough around Sunday/Monday. Either way, \nthis doesn't look particularly strong and the NBM forecast with \ncontinued dry conditions and temperatures near to slightly above \nnormal is reasonable.",
